ointsEligibleFlag":null,"ProductMedia":[],"InstructorsManualFlag":null,"InstructorsSolutionManualFlag":null,"ProductText":[],"AllowDeskExamCopyRequest":null,"EstimatedPagesFlag":null,"AddToCartFlag":null,"DisplayAuthorEditorFlag":null,"AMSPublication":null,"ShowMareNostrumLinkFlag":null},{"SeriesVolumes":[],"DOI":null,"Abstract":null,"EnhancedReferences":null,"UnenhancedReferences":null,"IssueNumber":1,"Subsystem":null,"Issues":[],"NonIssueArticles":[],"FileUrlHost":null,"HomeUrlHost":null,"OpenAccessType":null,"PrimaryFlag":null,"EOnlyFlag":null,"RetrieveAbstractsFlag":null,"MeetingTitle":null,"YearsUntilFree":0,"PISSN":null,"EISSN":null,"PublisherName":null,"ProductId":5864,"ProductCode":"MEMO/1/1","Title":null,"Subtitle":null,"AMSFlags":null,"MSCs":null,"CopyrightYear":"1950","PageCount":null,"CoverType":null,"SeriesCode":null,"Series":null,"Subseries":null,"ProductClassCode":null,"Volume":"1","ProductDescription":null,"Readership":null,"SmallImage":null,"LargeImage":null,"Alt1SmallImage":null,"Alt1LargeImage":null,"Alt2SmallImage":null,"Alt2LargeImage":null,"DefaultImageAltText":null,"Alt1ImageAltText":null,"Alt2ImageAltText":null,"Imprint":null,"PublisherText":null,"CooperationText":null,"OutOfStockReason":null,"NextReceiptDate":"0001-01-01T00:00:00","AdditionalAuthorText":null,"Distribution":null,"SeriesDistribution":null,"AdditionalSeriesText":null,"RelatedCustomers":[],"AddToCartItems":[],"TOCItems":[],"ReviewItems":[],"AwardItems":[],"RevisionItems":[],"FileItems":[],"CrossSellProducts":[],"SetProducts":[],"IncludedInSets":[],"AllowCopyrightPermissionFlag":null,"EpubISBN":null,"GetPermissionsItems":[],"ProductOptions":[],"PointsEligibleFlag":null,"ProductMedia":[],"InstructorsManualFlag":null,"InstructorsSolutionManualFlag":null,"ProductText":[],"AllowDeskExamCopyRequest":null,"EstimatedPagesFlag":null,"AddToCartFlag":null,"DisplayAuthorEditorFlag":null,"AMSPublication":null,"ShowMareNostrumLinkFlag":null}],"DOI":"10.1090/memo/1358","Abstract":"\u003Cp\u003EAn abelian network is a collection of communicating automata whose state transitions and message passing each satisfy a local commutativity condition. This paper is a continuation of the abelian networks series of Bond and Levine (2016), for which we extend the theory of abelian networks that halt on all inputs to networks that can run forever. A nonhalting abelian network can be realized as a discrete dynamical system in many different ways, depending on the update order. We show that certain features of the dynamics, such as minimal period length, have intrinsic definitions that do not require specifying an update order.\u003C/p\u003E\n\n\u003Cp\u003EWe give an intrinsic definition of the \u003Cem\u003Etorsion group\u003C/em\u003E of a finite irreducible (halting or nonhalting) abelian network, and show that it coincides with the critical group of Bond and Levine (2016) if the network is halting. We show that the torsion group acts freely on the set of invertible recurrent components of the trajectory digraph, and identify when this action is transitive.\u003C/p\u003E\n\n\u003Cp\u003EThis perspective leads to new results even in the classical case of sinkless rotor networks (deterministic analogues of random walks). In Holroyd et. al (2008) it was shown that the recurrent configurations of a sinkless rotor network with just one chip are precisely the unicycles (spanning subgraphs with a unique oriented cycle, with the chip on the cycle). We generalize this result to abelian mobile agent networks with any number of chips. We give formulas for generating series such as \\[\n \\sum _{n \\geq 1} r_n z^n = \\det (\\frac {1}{1-z}D - A ) \\] where $r_n$ is the number of recurrent chip-and-rotor configurations with $n$ chips; $D$ is the diagonal matrix of outdegrees, and $A$ is the adjacency matrix. A consequence is that the sequence $(r_n)_{n \\geq 1}$ completely determines the spectrum of the simple random walk on the network.\u003C/p\u003E","EnhancedReferences":"\n\n\